<div class="wrap page-head"><h1>Conferences</h1><p class="lede">Since 1997, an unofficial forum for the dialogue the official commissions carry on: lay people and clergy, Orthodox and Catholic, in the same room, at each other's liturgies.</p></div>
<section class="band" style="padding-top:1.5rem">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="event-card">
      <div>
        <p class="when">July 13 to 15, 2026 · St. John Paul II Shrine and Retreat Center, Washington, DC</p>
        <h3>Orientale Lumen XXX: “Ecumenism at a Crossroad: Consensus Achieved. What's Next?”</h3>
        <p>After three decades of Orientale Lumen conferences, the dialogue between Eastern Orthodox, Eastern Catholic, and Roman Catholic Christians has reached a critical stage. The agreed statements of Ravenna, Chieti, and Alexandria mark a significant convergence on primacy and synodality. This conference asks what the Church now understands by the primacy of the Bishop of Rome within a synodal ecclesiology, how that ministry can serve unity without diminishing the authority of patriarchs and synods, and how agreements reached in dialogue commissions are received in liturgy, pastoral life, and governance.</p>
        <p><strong>Speakers.</strong> Cardinal Seán O'Malley; Metropolitan Job of Pisidia (by video); Archbishop Flavio Pace; Archbishop Borys Gudziak (by video); Bishop Anthony Vrame; Bishop John Michael Botean; Archimandrite Cyril Hovorun (by video); Reader Jack Figel. Moderated by Dr. John Borelli, Georgetown University.</p>
        <p><strong>Co-sponsors.</strong> The Orientale Lumen Foundation, the Likoudis Legacy Foundation, the Washington Theological Consortium, and the Huffington Ecumenical Institute at Holy Cross Greek Orthodox School of Theology.</p>
